# YAGNI (You Aren't Gonna Need It)

Build the simplest thing that satisfies the task in front of you. Do not
build for a future task that doesn't exist yet.

- Don't add a feature, field, setting, or endpoint because it "might be
  useful later." Add it when a real task needs it, not before.
- Don't add configuration options, flags, or environment variables for
  behavior nothing currently uses. Every toggle is something that can be
  misconfigured.
- Prefer a plain, direct implementation over a general or "flexible" one.
  Don't build an abstraction layer, plugin system, or extensible framework
  to solve a problem a few lines of straightforward code already solves.
- Don't introduce a new dependency, library, or design pattern to handle a
  case that isn't real yet. Fewer dependencies means less to secure, update,
  and reason about.
- Don't create unused code paths: dead branches, unused parameters,
  commented-out alternatives "in case we go back to it." Delete instead of
  disabling.
- This applies to data models too — don't add speculative columns, tables,
  or relations for features that haven't been built. Extend the schema when
  the feature actually arrives.
- If you notice a genuine opportunity for something the project will likely
  need soon, say so in your task summary instead of building it — let me
  decide whether it's worth doing now.

## This does not override the safety floor

YAGNI is about deferring speculative features, not about deferring the
non-negotiables in `00-core-safety.mdc` (access control, server-side auth
checks, secrets handling). Those are needed for whatever exists today, not
for some future feature — so they're never "premature."
